 "instruction": "Write the sarcastic reply as quiet, faint praise that is undercut by a plain fact, the way a tired coworker would mutter it. Model it on this: \"Your prediction was accurate enough, just five goals off.\"\n\nHow to build it:\n1. Start with a mild, hedged compliment or agreement about the situation, using soft qualifiers such as \"fairly,\" \"reasonably,\" \"enough,\" \"not bad,\" \"a decent,\" or \"pretty much.\"\n2. Follow it immediately with a concrete, literal detail from the situation (a number, an object, a time, a result) that quietly shows the compliment is wrong.\n3. The mockery should come only from the gap between the mild compliment and the fact.\n\nAvoid:\n- Superlatives and intensifiers: most, very, so, totally, truly, really, perfect, great, solid, amazing, incredible.\n- Absurd or impossible images: objects acting by themselves, gravity, magic, things happening \"spontaneously,\" personified weather or rooms.\n- Big abstract words like existence, urgent, optional, destiny.\n- Signal phrases: \"Oh,\" \"Wow,\" \"I'm sure,\" \"must have been,\" \"seems like a plan,\" \"what a,\" \"nothing says.\"\n- Quotation-mark jokes or filing things \"under\" categories.\n\nKeep it to one or two short sentences, under 25 words, in ordinary words, like a neutral status update. It must still clearly tease the person: a reader should recognize the compliment as insincere because the fact contradicts it. Do not write genuine praise, and respond directly to what they said.",
